Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.5. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, at any time prior to the Closing Date, the Company Board may make a Company Recommendation Change <omitted> if, and only if, <omitted> (ii) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and strategic advisors, that the failure to make the Company Recommendation Change would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, (iii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board’s intention to make a Company Recommendation Change not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the third Business Day after Parent receives such written notice and (iv) the decision to make a Company Recommendation Change is in connection with an Acquisition Proposal or with the Company’s intent to terminate this Agreement to enter into an Alternative Agreement, and the Company shall have complied with clauses (1) through (4), as follows: (1) prior to giving effect to clauses (2) through (4), the Company Board shall have determined that such Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, (2) the Company shall have made available to Parent the material terms and conditions of such Acquisition Proposal and copies of all written communications relating to such Acquisition Proposal in accordance with Section 9.5(b), (3) the Company shall have negotiated in good faith with Parent, to the extent that Parent desires to negotiate, during the three Business Day period provided in the foregoing clause (iii) of this Section 9.5(c) with respect to such proposed revisions to this Agreement or other proposals made by Parent, if any, so that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer constitute a Superior Proposal, (4) after considering the results of negotiations with Parent and taking into account the proposals made by Parent, if any, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, strategic advisors, and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, the Company Board shall have determined in good faith that such Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal, and, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, that the failure to make the Company Recommendation Change would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law. For clarity, the provisions of this Section 9.5(c) shall also apply to any amendment to the financial terms or any other material amendment to any Acquisition Proposal (except that any reference to three Business Days shall instead be two Business Days) or any successive Acquisition Proposals. (Pages 88-89)\nAnswer:
Choices:

