Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 6.4. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (c) The Company Board, at any time prior to the Company Stockholder Meeting, may make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change if, and only if, (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor, that the failure to make the Company Adverse Recommendation Change would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable law, (ii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board’s intention to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change at least three Business Days in advance, and (iii) if the decision is in connection with an Acquisition Proposal, the Company Board shall have determined in good faith, after considering the advice of its outside legal counsel and financial advisor, that such Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. The Company shall have negotiated in good faith with Parent during the three Business Day period to allow Parent to propose adjustments to this Agreement such that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer constitute a Superior Proposal. (d) After considering any adjustments proposed by Parent, the Company Board shall determine in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor, that the failure to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change would still be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable law. The Company Board’s decision must be based on a comprehensive review of the proposal, including its financial terms, potential legal implications, and the strategic fit within the Company’s long-term goals. (Pages 63-64)\nAnswer:
